---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import { site } from '../site';

const description =
  'A decade of software engineering, including nearly five years leading ' +
  'accessibility work at Meta across web, Android and iOS.';
---

<BaseLayout title="About" description={description}>
  <div class="page region">
    <p class="eyebrow">About</p>
    <h1>Carmen Krol</h1>

    <div class="prose about">
      <p class="about__lede">
        I’m a software engineer with more than a decade of experience building
        software, including nearly five years dedicated to accessibility at
        Meta.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’ve worked with product teams to understand accessibility requirements,
        solve implementation challenges, and make accessibility a more practical
        part of software development.
      </p>

      <h2>What I actually do</h2>
      <p>
        I enjoy the intersection of accessibility and engineering: understanding
        why something doesn’t work for someone using assistive technology,
        figuring out how to fix it, and helping prevent similar problems from
        happening again.
      </p>
      <p>
        My experience includes working with engineers and product teams across
        web, Android, and iOS. I’ve helped address accessibility issues,
        contributed to accessible components and design systems, and built tools
        to make accessibility testing and remediation more effective.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’m particularly interested in the practical questions that come up
        during development: How should this component behave? What does a screen
        reader need to communicate? Which accessibility requirements can be
        addressed in the code, and how can teams catch problems earlier?
      </p>

      <h2>How I work</h2>
      <p>
        I believe accessibility is most effective when it’s part of how software
        is built—not something added at the end.
      </p>
      <p>
        That means combining automated tools with manual testing, including
        keyboard and assistive technology testing. It means looking beyond
        individual pages to understand the components and implementation
        decisions that may be causing problems.
      </p>
      <p>
        And it means helping engineers understand why something needs to change,
        so they can apply that knowledge to the next feature they build.
      </p>

      <h2>Background</h2>
      <p>
        I spent more than a decade working as a software engineer, most recently
        at Meta, where I was a tech lead on the central accessibility team for
        nearly five years.
      </p>
      <p>
        My work there included helping product teams build and remediate
        accessible experiences across web, Android, and iOS; developing
        accessible components and testing tools; and exploring AI-based
        approaches to identifying and addressing accessibility issues.
      </p>
      <p>
        I’m now taking time to explore independent consulting and other
        opportunities to apply my engineering and accessibility experience. I
        also have{' '}
        <a
          href="https://www.accessibilityassociation.org/cpacc"
          target="_blank"
        >
          CPACC certification provided by IAAP
        </a>
        .
      </p>
      <p>
        Before software engineering, I obtained my masters degree in Science
        &amp; Technology Studies at Cornell University, where I researched how
        users interact with technology in different cultural and social
        settings.
      </p>

      <h2>Elsewhere</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <a href={site.linkedInUrl} target="_blank">
            LinkedIn
          </a>
        </li>
        <li>
          <a href={site.repoUrl} rel="noopener" target="_blank">
            GitHub
          </a>
        </li>
      </ul>

      <p>
        The quickest way to reach me is <a href="/contact/">the contact form</a>
        , or <a href={`mailto:${site.email}`}>{site.email}</a> if you would
        rather email.
      </p>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>

<style>
  .about__lede {
    font-size: var(--step-1);
    color: var(--muted);
  }

  .about h2 {
    font-size: var(--step-2);
  }
</style>
